    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nIn this topic, we'll practice using the method of factoring to evaluate an expression given a linear equation containing binomials.\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Equations With Binomials on One Side\n\nExample Prompt:\nIf [MATH: 2(2z + 5) + 8(2z + 5) = 120,] what is the value of [MATH: 2z + 5?]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe are given the equation [MATH: 2(2z + 5) + 8(2z + 5) = 120] and are asked to find the value of [MATH: 2z + 5.]\n\nWe begin by combining like terms:\n[MATH: 2(2z + 5) + 8(2z + 5)|= 120; (2 + 8)(2z + 5)|= 120; 10(2z + 5)|= 120]\n\nNow we divide both sides by [MATH: 10] to isolate [MATH: 2z + 5{:}]\n[MATH: 2z + 5|= (120)/(10); = 12]\n\nTherefore, the value of [MATH: 2z + 5] is [MATH: 12.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: 4(y + 3) + 6(y + 3) = 50,] what is the value of [MATH: y + 3?]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 10]\nb. [MATH: 3]\nc. [MATH: 7]\nd. [MATH: 5]\ne. [MATH: 8]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: 2(2z + 5) + 8(2z + 5) = 120,] what is the value of [MATH: 2z + 5?]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 6]\nb. [MATH: 8]\nc. [MATH: 10]\nd. [MATH: 14]\ne. [MATH: 12]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Equations With Binomials on Both Sides\n\nExample Prompt:\nIf [MATH: 7(2q - 3) = 5(2q - 3) + 18,] what is the value of [MATH: 2q - 3?]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe are given the equation [MATH: 7(2q - 3) = 5(2q - 3) + 18] and are asked to find the value of [MATH: 2q - 3.]\n\nWe begin by combining like terms:\n[MATH: 7(2q \u2212 3)|= 5(2q \u2212 3) + 18; 7(2q \u2212 3) \u2212 5(2q \u2212 3)|= 18; (7 \u2212 5)(2q \u2212 3)|= 18; 2(2q \u2212 3)|= 18]\n\nNext, we divide both sides by [MATH: 2] to isolate [MATH: 2q - 3{:}]\n[MATH: 2q \u2212 3|= 9]\n\nSo, the value of [MATH: 2q - 3] is [MATH: 9.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: 7(y \u2212 2) = 5(y \u2212 2) + 16,] what is the value of [MATH: y \u2212 2?]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 8]\nb. [MATH: 20]\nc. [MATH: 18]\nd. [MATH: 6]\ne. [MATH: 10]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: 7(2q \u2212 3) = 5(2q \u2212 3) + 18,] what is the value of [MATH: 2q \u2212 3?]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 6]\nb. [MATH: 8]\nc. [MATH: 10]\nd. [MATH: 9]\ne. [MATH: 7]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Equations With Binomials and Constants on Both Sides\n\nExample Prompt:\nIf [MATH: 9 - 7(3b + 2) = 6 - 5(3b + 2),] then find [MATH: 3b+2.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe are given the equation\n[MATH: 9 - 7(3b + 2) = 6 - 5(3b + 2).]\n\nNotice that both sides contain the same binomial expression [MATH: (3b + 2).] We solve by moving all terms involving [MATH: (3b + 2)] to one side and constants to the other:\n[MATH: 9 \u2212 7(3b + 2)|= 6 \u2212 5(3b + 2); \u2212 7(3b + 2) + 5(3b + 2)|= 6 \u2212 9; (\u2212 7 + 5)(3b + 2)|= \u2212 3; (\u2212 2)(3b + 2)|= \u2212 3; 3b + 2|= (3)/(2)]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: 5(6 \u2212 2y) + 7 = 3(6 \u2212 2y) + 17,] what is the value of [MATH: 6 \u2212 2y?]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 3]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 2]\nc. [MATH: 5]\nd. [MATH: 10]\ne. [MATH: 2]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: 3 \u2212 5(1 + 2z) = 9 \u2212 6(1 + 2z),] then [MATH: 1 + 2z =][FREE_ENTRY 1]."
